# Halal Cart-Style Chicken and Rice
*Updated recipe by J. Kenji López-Alt — [watch on YouTube](https://youtu.be/K0wkS3xefZE)*

A one-pot meal inspired by the classic New York halal carts (think: the Halal Guys on 53rd & 6th in Midtown) — chopped chicken over yellow rice, drenched in white sauce and hot sauce, with iceberg, tomato, and onion. First published on Serious Eats around 2009–2010, this is the updated version.

---

## Chicken Marinade
*(for ~2 lbs boneless, skinless chicken thighs)*

- 2 tbsp fresh lemon juice
- ¼ tsp ground coriander
- 1 tbsp dried oregano
- 3 garlic cloves (minced or grated)
- ¼ cup olive oil (extra virgin or light)
- Salt, black pepper, and MSG to taste (it should be relatively salty — it doubles as a brine)

**Method:** Combine all marinade ingredients. Use half on the chicken and reserve the other half. Marinate 1–4 hours — **do not marinate overnight**, as the lemon juice will make the meat chalky. Trim the chicken of excess fat, but leave some for flavor and to render into the rice.

---

## Cooking the Chicken

1. Heat a large skillet (cast iron, stainless, or titanium) over medium-high heat with a drizzle of olive oil.
2. Sear chicken in a single layer until well-browned on both sides (aim for ~300–307°F pan temp).
3. Cook to an internal temp of 165–170°F+ (thighs are forgiving).
4. Rest, then chop into ¼–½ inch nubs.
5. At the end, reheat the chopped chicken in the **reserved raw marinade** with a splash of extra lemon juice, a drizzle of good olive oil, and final seasoning adjustments.

---

## Yellow Rice
*(cooked in the same pan to pick up the chicken fond and rendered fat)*

- 1½ cups long-grain rice (basmati or similar), washed and drained
- ½ tsp ground cumin (or whole cumin, bloomed in fat)
- ½ tsp+ turmeric
- ~½ tbsp butter (less than the original recipe since chicken fat is already in the pan)
- 2½ cups low-sodium chicken broth
- Salt, black pepper, MSG (go light — storebought broth adds saltiness)

**Method:**
1. In the same skillet used for chicken, bloom the cumin and turmeric in the residual chicken fat and butter over medium heat.
2. Add the drained rice and stir to coat.
3. Add broth, season, and bring to a boil.
4. Reduce to a bare simmer, cover, and cook 15 minutes.
5. Remove from heat and let rest, still covered, 10–15 minutes.
6. Fluff and adjust seasoning.

---

## White Sauce

- ½ cup Greek yogurt
- ½ cup mayonnaise
- 1 tbsp sugar
- 2 tbsp distilled white vinegar
- 1 tsp lemon juice
- Plenty of black pepper
- Salt and MSG (roughly 8:1 ratio)
- Chopped fresh parsley (or dill, or omit)

**Method:** Whisk everything together until smooth. Taste and adjust acid and seasoning.

---

## Hot Sauce

Kenji uses a combination of:
- **Mina harissa** (jarred) — for body and depth
- **Ghost pepper hot sauce** (e.g., Coutino Saskco ghost chili) — for vinegary heat

Alternatives: shatta sauce, or any chunkier dried chili sauce. It should be vinegary and spicy.

---

## Toppings

- Shredded iceberg lettuce (cut into chunky threads)
- Rough-chopped Roma tomatoes (or cherry tomatoes)
- Chopped onion *(soak in warm water for a few minutes to tame the pungency)*
- Pita bread (optional, on the side)

---

## To Serve

Plate with **~70% rice**, pile on the chicken, add the veggies, and drizzle generously with both white sauce and hot sauce. Everything should meld together into one saucy, spiced pile.

